#!/usr/bin/env python3
# mise description="Measure a cloud orchestrator dispatching to the local model against a dispatch-disabled control. Usage: mise run bench-hybrid --rung N [--arm hybrid|control] [--samples 3]"
"""Run the bench/specs/escalation.md ladder through nav-pilot, two arms, and refuse to
record anything we cannot stand behind.

Every invalid measurement this week looked exactly like a valid one, and each was
caught by eye, late. The list, and what now makes each impossible to record:

  * prompts carrying 8.5k tokens of instructions nobody chose   -> preflight 6
    asserts the instructions array and writes the inventory, with byte counts,
    into the results file
  * a run overlapping a 22 GB model download, six of eleven     -> preflight 3
    tasks timed out
  * a "cloud only" arm still dispatching, because disabling     -> preflight 6 runs
    dispatch needs `alpha local off`, not an omitted            `alpha local off`
    instruction                                                 and asserts clean
  * a "hybrid" arm that never reached the local server,         -> the post-launch
    because the binding lives in ~/.config/opencode and is      assert plus the
    written at launch by nav-pilot                              local_calls rule
  * timed-out tasks recording 0 turns, which reads as "the      -> nulls, never
    model did nothing" rather than "unknown"                    zeros
  * a results file merging two task sets because results were   -> one file per
    keyed only by model                                         (rung, arm)

Samples are driven through nav-pilot, never through bare `opencode`:

    nav-pilot --client opencode --model <cloud> -- --pure run --format json "<prompt>"

nav-pilot carries the cplt sandbox a real session has, starts the in-process loop
guard whose port the provider block points at, and writes the worker binding and
the dispatch fragment at launch. A bare `opencode` run has none of that: it either
bypasses the guard or cannot reach the local model at all, depending on whether a
launch happens to be live. That is how a "hybrid" arm measured the cloud.

So the config lifecycle is part of what we measure, not a precondition. The binding
and fragment must be ABSENT before a hybrid launch and PRESENT after it; for the
control they must be absent both times. A sample where that does not hold is not
recorded as a result.

No-GPU paths: `--help` and `--self-check`.
"""
